Paper:Rethinking the Evaluation of Secure Code Generation Shih-Chieh Dai, Jun Xu, Guanhong Tao

**Background & Motivation:**

Prior secure-code-generation approaches (e.g. fine-tuning-based or prompt/decoding-based) aim to reduce vulnerabilities in code generated by large language models (LLMs), such as unsafe patterns or insecure defaults. 

However, existing evaluations often treat security (vulnerability detection) and functionality (correctness / passing tests) separately, typically using different datasets for each. This separation hides potential trade-offs or regressions. 

Also, many studies rely on a single static analyzer (commonly CodeQL) to detect vulnerabilities — which may miss some issues or overstate security improvements.


**Contribution:**

It performs a comprehensive, systematic evaluation of four state-of-the-art secure code generation techniques (e.g. fine-tuning methods, decoding/prompting methods) under a joint evaluation: assessing both security and functionality on the same generated code. 


It uses multiple vulnerability detectors (three different static analyzers, not just CodeQL) and two different LLMs, to better capture a diverse range of potential security issues. 


It runs the evaluation on large-scale benchmarks/datasets (e.g. BigCodeBench and SecCodePLT+), avoiding small-sample bias that can afflict prior work.


Key Findings / Insights:
When evaluated for both security and functionality together, many of the secure-generation techniques perform much worse than previously reported. Some even degrade base-model performance by over 50%. 

In some cases, improvements in “security” come at the cost of correctness: generated code may pass fewer functionality tests, or be “garbage code” — i.e., irrelevant or nonfunctional code — or simply remove vulnerable lines (rather than fix them), undermining usefulness. 


The commonly used single-analyzer approach (e.g. just CodeQL) is insufficient: different static analyzers detect different subsets of vulnerabilities, so relying on one leads to overconfident assessments of “safe” code. 

**Implications & Critique**

The paper argues for more rigorous evaluation protocols: secure code generation should not be judged on security alone, but on security + functional correctness jointly, to ensure generated code is both secure and usable. 


It also suggests that evaluations should use multiple vulnerability detectors, ideally with complementary detection capabilities, to avoid blind spots. 


The findings warn that many “secure code generation” claims may be overstated under narrow evaluation regimes; future work needs to balance security, correctness, and real-world utility.


